 let's start with the cosine of  theta we'd find the cosine of theta as  let's take an angle  that's between two radiuses  in this unit circle  and one radius is going to be  the positive x-axis between zero and one  so one radius is going to be this line  here  and then the and then we have the angle  is the angle between that bit kind of  you could kind of view that as the base  radius and some other radius so let's  say this case right here  and this is this would be our angle  the unit circle definition tells us that  the cosine of this angle is equal to the  x coordinate where this radius  intersects the unit circle  and that the sine of this function is  equal to the y-coordinate where this  point intersects the unit circle so for  example this  in this case if you can read behind  my my line this says 30 degrees equals  pi over 6. so this angle right here  is 30 degrees  or pi over 6  radians  pi over 6  radians and what this definition tells  us is that the sine of 30 degrees is one  half  and that the cosine of 30 degrees  is square root of three over two  and what i want to show you is that this  unit circle definition actually  coincides with our sohcahtoa  definition  but then it extends it so let's see how  we can get from that sokatoa definition  to this unit circle definition and why  they're actually consistent with each  other so let me erase some of what i  have  written here let me get the eraser tool  i'm going to erase  what i had  so let me  go back to the pen tool  make it  small again  okay i think i'm all set  so let's let's go back to that that  theta let's say that this is  the theta  and we we as we said this angle is 30  degrees or pi over 6.  let's drop  a line from that point  to the x-axis and as we see this line is  perpendicular so this is a 90 degree  angle  this is a 90 degree angle  and if this is a 30 degree angle here  this is 30  right theta equals 30 that says theta  equals 30. this is 30 this is 90. what  is this angle this 